{title: Come, Thou Fount of Every Blessing}
{lyricist: Robert Robinson}
{composer: American folk melody}
{year: 1758}
{meta: tune-year 1813}
{key: D}
{time: 3/4}
{meta: tune NETTLETON}
{meta: meter 8.7.8.7 D}
{meta: variation The hymn, sung to NETTLETON}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: tags grace, praise}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
Come, Thou [D]Fount of [G]every [D]blessing,
Tune my [D]heart to [A]sing Thy [D]grace;
Streams of [D]mercy, [G]never [D]ceasing,
Call for [D]songs of [A]loudest [D]praise.
Teach me [D]some me[G]lodious [A]sonnet,
Sung by [D]flaming [G]tongues a[A]bove;
Praise the [D]mount! I'm [G]fixed up[D]on it,
Mount of [D]Thy re[A]deeming [D]love.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
Here I [D]raise mine [G]Ebe[D]nezer;
Hither [D]by Thy [A]help I'm [D]come;
And I [D]hope, by [G]Thy good [D]pleasure,
Safely [D]to ar[A]rive at [D]home.
Jesus [D]sought me [G]when a [A]stranger,
Wandering [D]from the [G]fold of [A]God;
He, to [D]rescue [G]me from [D]danger,
Inter[D]posed His [A]precious [D]blood.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
O to [D]grace how [G]great a [D]debtor
Daily [D]I'm con[A]strained to [D]be!
Let Thy [D]goodness, [G]like a [D]fetter,
Bind my [D]wandering [A]heart to [D]Thee.
Prone to [D]wander, [G]Lord, I [A]feel it,
Prone to [D]leave the [G]God I [A]love;
Here's my [D]heart, O [G]take and [D]seal it,
Seal it [D]for Thy [A]courts a[D]bove.
{end_of_verse}
